---
source: crates/standalone/fixture/src/lib.rs
---
Serve a built deploy the way production does.

\e[1mUsage\e[0m: \e[1m\e[36mink\e[0m \e[1m\e[36mserve\e[0m \e[36m[OPTIONS]\e[0m \e[36m[FOLDER]\e[0m

\e[1mExamples\e[0m
  \e[2m# \e[0mServe .ink/out of this folder on 127.0.0.1:8080
  \e[1m\e[36mink\e[0m \e[1m\e[36mserve\e[0m

  \e[2m# \e[0mServe .ink/out of another folder
  \e[1m\e[36mink\e[0m \e[1m\e[36m-C\e[0m garden \e[1m\e[36mserve\e[0m

  \e[2m# \e[0mSelf-host: serve, watch and build git sites from disk, restarting a crashed
  \e[2m# \e[0mloop three times
  \e[1m\e[36mink\e[0m \e[1m\e[36mserve\e[0m \e[1m\e[36m--data\e[0m state \e[1m\e[36m--git-scheme\e[0m file \e[1m\e[36m--restart-limit\e[0m 3

  \e[2m# \e[0mTurn on the API and send sign-in links by SMTP
  \e[1m\e[36mink\e[0m \e[1m\e[36mserve\e[0m \e[1m\e[36m--data\e[0m state \e[1m\e[36m--mail\e[0m smtp \e[1m\e[36m--mail-from\e[0m ink@example.org \e[1m\e[36m--smtp-host\e[0m mail.example.org

\e[1mArguments\e[0m
  \e[36m[FOLDER]\e[0m  A folder `\e[1m\e[36mink\e[0m \e[1m\e[36mbuild\e[0m` wrote into, or with `\e[1m\e[36m--host-mode\e[0m \e[1m\e[36mfolders\e[0m` a
            folder of them, one per host name \e[2m[default: .ink/out of the site]\e[0m

\e[1mServer\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --addr\e[0m \e[36m<ADDRESS>\e[0m         The address to listen on
                               \e[2m[default: 127.0.0.1:8080]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --host-mode\e[0m \e[36m<HOST_MODE>\e[0m  How a request's host picks a deploy
                               \e[2m[default: auto]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --data\e[0m \e[36m<DATA>\e[0m            Turns on the control plane and its API. Its
                               database, blobs, sealing key and the operator's
                               public keys live in this folder. Without it only
                               the folder is served
  \e[1m\e[36m    --api-host\e[0m \e[36m<API_HOST>\e[0m    The host name whose requests are API calls,
                               signed with a key (`\e[1m\e[36mink\e[0m \e[1m\e[36mlogin\e[0m`)
                               \e[2m[default: api.localhost]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --origin\e[0m \e[36m<ORIGIN>\e[0m        Where sites are served, for the addresses the API
                               reports (`\e[1m\e[36m<handle>.<host>\e[0m`) and the origin
                               passkeys are made for: give the address a browser
                               uses to reach this server \e[2m[default:\e[0m
                               \e[2m`http://localhost:`\e[0m \e[2mand\e[0m \e[2mthe\e[0m \e[2mlistening\e[0m \e[2mport]\e[0m

\e[1mLimits\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --api-body\e[0m \e[36m<API_BODY>\e[0m            The largest JSON request body, in bytes.
                                       \e[2m[default: 65536]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --upload-bytes\e[0m \e[36m<UPLOAD_BYTES>\e[0m    The largest uploaded archive, in bytes.
                                       \e[2m[default: 268435456]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --upload-files\e[0m \e[36m<UPLOAD_FILES>\e[0m    The most files in an uploaded archive.
                                       \e[2m[default: 10000]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --upload-file\e[0m \e[36m<UPLOAD_FILE>\e[0m      The largest file in an uploaded archive,
                                       in bytes. \e[2m[default: 67108864]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --upload-tree\e[0m \e[36m<UPLOAD_TREE>\e[0m      The largest tree `\e[1m\e[36mink\e[0m \e[1m\e[36mpush\e[0m` may send, in
                                       bytes; the tree is read whole.
                                       \e[2m[default: 8388608]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --upload-part\e[0m \e[36m<UPLOAD_PART>\e[0m      The most bytes of files in one `\e[1m\e[36mink\e[0m \e[1m\e[36mpush\e[0m`
                                       request, held in memory while it is
                                       checked and stored. \e[2m[default: 67108864]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --export-bytes\e[0m \e[36m<EXPORT_BYTES>\e[0m    The largest exported archive, in bytes.
                                       \e[2m[default: 268435456]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --poll-interval\e[0m \e[36m<POLL_INTERVAL>\e[0m  Seconds between polls of a git source. At
                                       least 10. \e[2m[default: 600]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --poll-max\e[0m \e[36m<POLL_MAX>\e[0m            Seconds a source that does not change is
                                       backed off to. \e[2m[default: 3600]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --abuse-limit\e[0m \e[36m<ABUSE_LIMIT>\e[0m      Abuse reports that may be sent per
                                       source, as `\e[1m\e[36m<count>/<window>\e[0m`. Reports
                                       take no account, so this is what bounds
                                       them. \e[2m[default: 5/1h]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_ABUSE_LIMIT]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --enrol-limit\e[0m \e[36m<ENROL_LIMIT>\e[0m      Asks to enrol a key that may be sent per
                                       source, as `\e[1m\e[36m<count>/<window>\e[0m`. They take
                                       no account, so this keeps one source from
                                       filling the queue of waiting keys.
                                       \e[2m[default: 5/1h]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_ENROL_LIMIT]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --handle-hold\e[0m \e[36m<DAYS>\e[0m             Days a handle is held after its site left
                                       it, so no one else can claim it; a
                                       verified owner's handle is held for good.
                                       \e[2m[default: 365]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_HANDLE_HOLD]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --limit-stored\e[0m \e[36m<BYTES>\e[0m           Bytes an account may store across its
                                       sites; a deploy over it is refused whole.
                                       \e[2m[default: 1073741824]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_LIMIT_STORED]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --limit-builds\e[0m \e[36m<N>\e[0m               Builds an account may start in 24 hours;
                                       a request over it is refused.
                                       \e[2m[default: 60]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_LIMIT_BUILDS]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --limit-sites\e[0m \e[36m<N>\e[0m                Sites an account may have. \e[2m[default: 10]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_LIMIT_SITES]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --limit-deploys\e[0m \e[36m<N>\e[0m              Deploys of a site that are kept, the live
                                       one included; older ones are removed with
                                       the files only they used. \e[2m[default: 20]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_LIMIT_DEPLOYS]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --remove-step\e[0m \e[36m<N>\e[0m                The most blobs one step of removing a
                                       site deletes. A removal runs in steps
                                       this size, so a site with many files
                                       never holds a request or a worker for
                                       long. \e[2m[default: 500]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_REMOVE_STEP]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --preview-days\e[0m \e[36m<DAYS>\e[0m            Days a preview of a branch is served
                                       before it ends and is swept. Previews are
                                       public to anyone with the address.
                                       \e[2m[default: 7]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_PREVIEW_DAYS]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --preview-branches\e[0m \e[36m<N>\e[0m           The most branches of one public git site
                                       the watch builds as previews, by name;
                                       `\e[1m\e[36m0\e[0m` builds none. The branch the site
                                       itself builds is not one of them.
                                       \e[2m[default: 10]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_PREVIEW_BRANCHES]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --limit-domains\e[0m \e[36m<N>\e[0m              The most custom domains one site has,
                                       proved and not. A claim of a name costs a
                                       row, so the number bounds what one site
                                       can ask for. \e[2m[default: 10]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_LIMIT_DOMAINS]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --limit-reports\e[0m \e[36m<N>\e[0m              The most abuse reports open at once, over
                                       every source. Anyone may send one, so the
                                       number bounds what many sources together
                                       can store; a report the operator actioned
                                       by suspending a site stops counting, and
                                       raising this is the only other way to
                                       make room. \e[2m[default: 10000]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_LIMIT_REPORTS]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --blob-grace\e[0m \e[36m<SECONDS>\e[0m           Seconds a blob that no deploy and no
                                       preview names stays before it is deleted.
                                       A build that skipped uploading a blob
                                       because the store held it keeps it until
                                       its deploy is recorded, so this must
                                       outlast that gap. \e[2m[default: 900]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_BLOB_GRACE]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --domain-recheck-days\e[0m \e[36m<DAYS>\e[0m     Days between two reads of the TXT record
                                       that proves a custom domain. A name whose
                                       record no longer carries its token stops
                                       resolving at once. \e[2m[default: 30]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_DOMAIN_RECHECK_DAYS]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --domain-lapse-days\e[0m \e[36m<DAYS>\e[0m       Days a custom domain that failed its
                                       check keeps its name before any site may
                                       claim it. The owner may publish the
                                       record again in that time and the name
                                       serves again. \e[2m[default: 14]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_DOMAIN_LAPSE_DAYS]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --limit-build-time\e[0m \e[36m<SECONDS>\e[0m     Seconds one build may take: a worker
                                       holds a request for no longer than this,
                                       so a build that runs past it is given to
                                       another. \e[2m[default: 600]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_LIMIT_BUILD_TIME]\e[0m

\e[1mMail\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --mail\e[0m \e[36m<MAIL>\e[0m                    Where sign-in mail goes \e[2m[default: stdout]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_MAIL]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --mail-from\e[0m \e[36m<MAIL_FROM>\e[0m          The address mail is sent from. Required
                                       with `\e[1m\e[36m--mail\e[0m \e[1m\e[36msmtp\e[0m` \e[2m[env: INK_MAIL_FROM]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --smtp-host\e[0m \e[36m<SMTP_HOST>\e[0m          The SMTP server's host name
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_SMTP_HOST]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --smtp-port\e[0m \e[36m<SMTP_PORT>\e[0m          The SMTP server's port. \e[2m[default:\e[0m \e[2m587,\e[0m \e[2mor\e[0m
                                       \e[2m465\e[0m \e[2mwith\e[0m \e[2m`--smtp-security\e[0m \e[2mimplicit`]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_SMTP_PORT]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --smtp-security\e[0m \e[36m<SMTP_SECURITY>\e[0m  How the SMTP connection is secured
                                       \e[2m[default: starttls]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_SMTP_SECURITY]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --smtp-user\e[0m \e[36m<SMTP_USER>\e[0m          The SMTP username \e[2m[env: INK_SMTP_USER]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --smtp-password-file\e[0m \e[36m<SMTP_PASSWORD_FILE>\e[0m
        A file holding the SMTP password. `\e[1m\e[36mINK_SMTP_PASSWORD\e[0m` holds it instead;
        there is no flag for the password itself, which a process list would
        show \e[2m[env: INK_SMTP_PASSWORD_FILE]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --login-base\e[0m \e[36m<LOGIN_BASE>\e[0m        Where the link in a message points; the
                                       token is added as one more path segment.
                                       \e[2m[default:\e[0m \e[2mthe\e[0m \e[2mdashboard's\e[0m \e[2m`/login`\e[0m \e[2mon\e[0m \e[2mthe\e[0m
                                       \e[2mAPI's\e[0m \e[2maddress]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_LOGIN_BASE]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --outbox\e[0m \e[36m<OUTBOX>\e[0m                How many messages wait to be sent before
                                       more are dropped. \e[2m[default: 256]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_OUTBOX]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --courier-rest\e[0m \e[36m<COURIER_REST>\e[0m    How long the mail sender rests when
                                       nothing waits. \e[2m[default: 1s]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_COURIER_REST]\e[0m

\e[1mSign-in\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --login-get-signs-in\e[0m             Whether opening the link with a GET signs
                                       in, which a mail scanner can do for you.
                                       Without it a GET only looks, and a page
                                       in front of the API POSTs
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_LOGIN_GET_SIGNS_IN]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --login-ttl\e[0m \e[36m<LOGIN_TTL>\e[0m          How long a sign-in link works.
                                       \e[2m[default: 15m]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_LOGIN_TTL]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --admin-ttl\e[0m \e[36m<ADMIN_TTL>\e[0m          How long the first-run link works.
                                       \e[2m[default: 24h]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_ADMIN_TTL]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --session-expiry\e[0m \e[36m<SESSION_EXPIRY>\e[0m
        How a session ends \e[2m[default: sliding]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_SESSION_EXPIRY]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --session-idle\e[0m \e[36m<SESSION_IDLE>\e[0m    How long a sliding session may sit
                                       unused. \e[2m[default: 7d]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_SESSION_IDLE]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --session-limit\e[0m \e[36m<SESSION_LIMIT>\e[0m  The longest a session lasts.
                                       \e[2m[default: 30d]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_SESSION_LIMIT]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --signup\e[0m \e[36m<SIGNUP>\e[0m                Who may make an account by signing in:
                                       `\e[1m\e[36mfirst\e[0m` (the first, by the first-run
                                       link), `\e[1m\e[36mopen\e[0m` (any address that receives
                                       mail) or `\e[1m\e[36mclosed\e[0m`. \e[2m[default: first]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_SIGNUP]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --rate-address\e[0m \e[36m<RATE_ADDRESS>\e[0m    Requests for a link per address, as
                                       `\e[1m\e[36m<count>/<window>\e[0m`. \e[2m[default: 5/1h]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_RATE_ADDRESS]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --rate-source\e[0m \e[36m<RATE_SOURCE>\e[0m      Requests for a link per source (an IPv4
                                       address or an IPv6 /64). \e[2m[default: 20/1h]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_RATE_SOURCE]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --rate-tracked\e[0m \e[36m<RATE_TRACKED>\e[0m    How many addresses and sources the limits
                                       remember at once. \e[2m[default: 10000]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_RATE_TRACKED]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --cookie-name\e[0m \e[36m<COOKIE_NAME>\e[0m      The session cookie's name. \e[2m[default:\e[0m
                                       \e[2m`__Host-ink-session`,\e[0m \e[2mor\e[0m \e[2m`ink-session`\e[0m \e[2mon\e[0m
                                       \e[2mplain\e[0m \e[2mhttp]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_COOKIE_NAME]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --cookie-unprefixed\e[0m              Take a `\e[1m\e[36m--cookie-name\e[0m` that does not
                                       start with `\e[1m\e[36m__Host-\e[0m` on a `\e[1m\e[36mSecure\e[0m`
                                       cookie. Without it such a name is
                                       refused, because a `\e[1m\e[36m__Host-\e[0m` name is what
                                       stops a sibling site from setting the
                                       cookie \e[2m[env: INK_COOKIE_UNPREFIXED]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --cookie-path\e[0m \e[36m<COOKIE_PATH>\e[0m      The session cookie's path. \e[2m[default: /]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_COOKIE_PATH]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --rp-id\e[0m \e[36m<RP_ID>\e[0m                  The relying party id of passkeys: a host
                                       name the dashboard is served on or above.
                                       A passkey made under one id never works
                                       under another. \e[2m[default: the API host]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_RP_ID]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --rp-origin\e[0m \e[36m<RP_ORIGIN>\e[0m          An origin the dashboard is served from,
                                       which passkey ceremonies must come from.
                                       Repeat for more than one.
                                       \e[2m[default: the origin of the API host]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_RP_ORIGIN]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --allow-origin\e[0m \e[36m<ALLOW_ORIGIN>\e[0m    An origin besides the API's own that may
                                       post state-changing requests with a
                                       session cookie, such as a dashboard
                                       served on another host. Repeat for more
                                       than one \e[2m[env: INK_ALLOW_ORIGIN]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --challenge-limit\e[0m \e[36m<CHALLENGE_LIMIT>\e[0m
        Passkey sign-ins that may start per source, as `\e[1m\e[36m<count>/<window>\e[0m`.
        \e[2m[default: 60/1h]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_CHALLENGE_LIMIT]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --recovery-limit\e[0m \e[36m<RECOVERY_LIMIT>\e[0m
        Recovery codes that may be tried per source, as `\e[1m\e[36m<count>/<window>\e[0m`.
        \e[2m[default: 10/1h]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_RECOVERY_LIMIT]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --step-up-window\e[0m \e[36m<STEP_UP_WINDOW>\e[0m
        How recent a passkey sign-in must be to add or remove a passkey, approve
        a key or make recovery codes. \e[2m[default: 5m]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_STEP_UP_WINDOW]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --forwarded-hops\e[0m \e[36m<FORWARDED_HOPS>\e[0m
        How many reverse proxies of yours sit in front of the server. With it,
        the client's address is read from `\e[1m\e[36mX-Forwarded-For\e[0m`, that many entries
        from the end, and a request without it is refused.
        \e[2m[default: off, the connection's address is used]\e[0m
        \e[2m[env: INK_FORWARDED_HOPS]\e[0m

\e[1mKeys\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --audience\e[0m \e[36m<AUDIENCE>\e[0m            The authority a signature is made for, as
                                       a client writes it: `\e[1m\e[36mhost[:port]\e[0m` of the
                                       API. Not the `\e[1m\e[36mHost\e[0m` header, so a proxy
                                       cannot choose it. \e[2m[default:\e[0m \e[2m`--api-host`\e[0m
                                       \e[2mand\e[0m \e[2mthe\e[0m \e[2mport\e[0m \e[2mof\e[0m \e[2m`--addr`]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_AUDIENCE]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --auth-skew\e[0m \e[36m<AUTH_SKEW>\e[0m          Seconds either side of the server's time
                                       a signed request may be, and the quiet
                                       after a restart. At most 300.
                                       \e[2m[default: 60]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_AUTH_SKEW]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --auth-nonces\e[0m \e[36m<AUTH_NONCES>\e[0m      How many request nonces are remembered
                                       for one key at once. \e[2m[default: 1024]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_AUTH_NONCES]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --key-device\e[0m \e[36m<KEY_DEVICE>\e[0m        How long a device key lives.
                                       \e[2m[default: 180d]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_KEY_DEVICE]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --key-deploy\e[0m \e[36m<KEY_DEPLOY>\e[0m        How long a deploy key lives.
                                       \e[2m[default: 90d]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_KEY_DEPLOY]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --key-ceiling\e[0m \e[36m<KEY_CEILING>\e[0m      The longest a key may be asked to live.
                                       \e[2m[default: 365d]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_KEY_CEILING]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --enrol-ttl\e[0m \e[36m<ENROL_TTL>\e[0m          How long a person has to approve a key.
                                       \e[2m[default: 10m]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_ENROL_TTL]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --enrol-guesses\e[0m \e[36m<ENROL_GUESSES>\e[0m  How many wrong codes close enrolling.
                                       \e[2m[default: 5]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_ENROL_GUESSES]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --enrol-waiting\e[0m \e[36m<ENROL_WAITING>\e[0m  How many keys may wait for approval at
                                       once. \e[2m[default: 20]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_ENROL_WAITING]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --enrol-closed\e[0m \e[36m<ENROL_CLOSED>\e[0m    How long enrolling stays closed.
                                       \e[2m[default: 1m]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_ENROL_CLOSED]\e[0m

\e[1mHosting\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --site-ttl\e[0m \e[36m<SITE_TTL>\e[0m            Seconds a site's live deploy is
                                       remembered before the control plane is
                                       asked again: a new deploy or a rollback
                                       shows within this long. The default asks
                                       every time, since the lookup and the
                                       control plane share one process and one
                                       database, so there is no staleness to
                                       trade for speed; raise it only to spare
                                       the database a read per request.
                                       \e[2m[default: 0]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --site-deploys\e[0m \e[36m<SITE_DEPLOYS>\e[0m    How many decoded deploys the lookup
                                       keeps. \e[2m[default: 64]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --poll-lookout\e[0m \e[36m<POLL_LOOKOUT>\e[0m    Seconds before the watch looks again for
                                       a site it has not seen move, at most; so
                                       a new site is first polled within this
                                       long. At least 1. \e[2m[default: 5]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --worker-lease\e[0m \e[36m<WORKER_LEASE>\e[0m    Seconds a worker holds a build request
                                       before the queue gives it to another.
                                       \e[2m[default: 900]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --worker-idle\e[0m \e[36m<WORKER_IDLE>\e[0m      Seconds a worker rests when the queue is
                                       empty. \e[2m[default: 2]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --restart-limit\e[0m \e[36m<RESTART_LIMIT>\e[0m  How many times in a row the watch or the
                                       worker may crash before it is left
                                       stopped. \e[2m[default: 5]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --restart-delay\e[0m \e[36m<RESTART_DELAY>\e[0m  Seconds to wait before a crashed loop is
                                       started again. \e[2m[default: 2]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --restart-healthy\e[0m \e[36m<RESTART_HEALTHY>\e[0m
        Seconds a loop must run for a crash after it to count as the first
        again. \e[2m[default: 60]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --git-scheme\e[0m \e[36m<GIT_SCHEMES>\e[0m       A kind of git address a site may be added
                                       with and the builder fetches; repeat for
                                       more. \e[2m[default: https]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --git-cache\e[0m \e[36m<GIT_CACHE>\e[0m          Where clones of git sites are kept.
                                       \e[2m[default: <data>/git]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --git-time\e[0m \e[36m<GIT_TIME>\e[0m            Seconds a fetch and read of a git source
                                       may take. \e[2m[default: 120]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --git-connect\e[0m \e[36m<GIT_CONNECT>\e[0m      Seconds to wait for a connection to an
                                       https git remote. \e[2m[default: 10]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --git-request\e[0m \e[36m<GIT_REQUEST>\e[0m      Seconds one https request to a git remote
                                       may take in all, its body included: also
                                       the longest a pack may take to arrive.
                                       \e[2m[default: 120]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --git-redirects\e[0m \e[36m<GIT_REDIRECTS>\e[0m  Which redirects of an https git remote
                                       are followed; one that leaves https never
                                       is. \e[2m[default: first]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --git-ca\e[0m \e[36m<GIT_CAS>\e[0m               A PEM file of certificate authorities to
                                       trust beside the bundled web roots, for a
                                       forge with a private authority; repeat
                                       for more. Certificates are always
                                       verified
  \e[1m\e[36m    --resolver\e[0m \e[36m<RESOLVER>\e[0m            How the TXT records that prove a custom
                                       domain are read: `\e[1m\e[36msystem\e[0m` asks
                                       nameservers, `\e[1m\e[36moffline\e[0m` asks none, so a
                                       claim is made and a proof says why it
                                       cannot be checked. \e[2m[default: system]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_RESOLVER]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --resolver-server\e[0m \e[36m<ADDR>\e[0m         A nameserver to ask, as `\e[1m\e[36mADDRESS\e[0m` or
                                       `\e[1m\e[36mADDRESS:PORT\e[0m`, in order; repeat for
                                       more. \e[2m[default:\e[0m \e[2mthe\e[0m \e[2m`nameserver`\e[0m \e[2mlines\e[0m \e[2mof\e[0m
                                       \e[2m`--resolver-file`]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_RESOLVER_SERVERS]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --resolver-confirm\e[0m \e[36m<ADDR>\e[0m        A second set of nameservers, as `\e[1m\e[36mADDRESS\e[0m`
                                       or `\e[1m\e[36mADDRESS:PORT\e[0m`; repeat for more. When
                                       it is set, a proof record is trusted only
                                       when both sets give the same records, so
                                       one forged answer on the way to one
                                       nameserver proves nothing. DNSSEC is not
                                       checked either way. \e[2m[default: none]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_RESOLVER_CONFIRM]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --resolver-file\e[0m \e[36m<PATH>\e[0m           The file the system lists its nameservers
                                       in. \e[2m[default: /etc/resolv.conf]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_RESOLVER_FILE]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --resolver-timeout\e[0m \e[36m<SECONDS>\e[0m     Seconds one lookup of a proof record may
                                       take in all, shared by the nameservers in
                                       turn. 1 to 60. \e[2m[default: 5]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_RESOLVER_TIMEOUT]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --forge-hooks\e[0m                    Take webhooks from forges at `\e[1m\e[36mPOST\e[0m
                                       \e[1m\e[36m/v1/hooks/{github,forgejo,gitlab}\e[0m`, and
                                       let a site claim and prove its
                                       repository, which the server reads from
                                       the forge over https. Off by default
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_FORGE_HOOKS]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --forge-instances\e[0m \e[36m<FILE>\e[0m         A TOML file of the Forgejo instances this
                                       server talks to, in the format of the
                                       built-in list (Codeberg). A repository on
                                       a host that is on no list is refused
                                       before anything is sent
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_FORGE_INSTANCES]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --forge-gitlab\e[0m \e[36m<HOST>\e[0m            The GitLab hosts this server talks to;
                                       repeat or separate with commas.
                                       \e[2m[default: gitlab.com]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_FORGE_GITLAB]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --forge-route\e[0m \e[36m<HOST=ADDR>\e[0m        Send a forge's name to an address instead
                                       of the one DNS gives, as
                                       `\e[1m\e[36mHOST=ADDRESS:PORT\e[0m`: for a mirror, or a
                                       test. The certificate is still checked
                                       for the name \e[2m[env: INK_FORGE_ROUTES]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --forge-timeout\e[0m \e[36m<SECONDS>\e[0m        Seconds one call to a forge may take in
                                       all. \e[2m[default: 10]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_FORGE_TIMEOUT]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --forge-reply\e[0m \e[36m<BYTES>\e[0m            The most bytes of a forge's reply that
                                       are read. \e[2m[default: 1048576]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_FORGE_REPLY]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --forge-body\e[0m \e[36m<BYTES>\e[0m             The most bytes of a webhook body that are
                                       read. \e[2m[default: 5242880]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_FORGE_BODY]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --forge-window\e[0m \e[36m<SECONDS>\e[0m         Seconds a delivery id is remembered, so
                                       the same delivery is taken once.
                                       \e[2m[default: 600]\e[0m \e[2m[env: INK_FORGE_WINDOW]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --forge-remembered\e[0m \e[36m<N>\e[0m           How many delivery ids of one repository
                                       are remembered at once. \e[2m[default: 10000]\e[0m
                                       \e[2m[env: INK_FORGE_REMEMBERED]\e[0m

\e[1mNotes\e[0m
  Unlike preview, this serves what `\e[1m\e[36mink\e[0m \e[1m\e[36mbuild\e[0m` already wrote, through the same
  edge code production runs: conditional and range requests, the host and path
  refusals, the request and response budgets, and the manifest's headers as they
  are in production.

\e[1mExit codes\e[0m
  0  Stopped with Ctrl-C
  2  The folder is no deploy, or the address cannot be used

\e[1mEnvironment\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36mINK_SMTP_PASSWORD\e[0m  The SMTP password for --mail smtp; never a flag, so it
                     stays out of process lists. Every other sign-in setting is
                     a flag with its own INK_ variable

